# CoolNasheed · environment
#
# Copy to `.env` at the repository root and fill in the two values. Both are public by
# design: the `anon` key is the key a browser is meant to hold, and everything it can
# reach is fenced by Row Level Security. Never put the `service_role` key in this file —
# it belongs to the Edge Functions, which read it from the platform.
#
# With nothing set, the app boots in demo mode: the bundled catalogue plays, the lyrics
# still sync, and every account-gated action explains that it needs a project.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the client (public)
# Project settings → API → Project URL
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
# Project settings → API → anon public
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ------------------------------------------------------- the Edge Functions (server-side)
# Supabase injects all three into a deployed function automatically. These are only
# here for `supabase functions serve` in a terminal of your own, and for reference:
#   SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-REF.supabase.co
#   SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<anon key>
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<service_role key — never in the browser, never committed>

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- deploying, once
# Not read by the app; handy to keep next to the keys so `npm run deploy` has a target.
#   supabase login                       # opens a browser, stores a token locally
#   supabase link --project-ref YOUR-REF
#   supabase db push                     # runs supabase/migrations/*
#   psql "$SUPABASE_DB_URL" -f supabase/seed.sql   # the catalogue (optional, idempotent)
#   supabase functions deploy            # all six
#
# Then in the dashboard:
#   Authentication → Providers → Email: on. Turn "Confirm email" off for a demo, on for
#     anything public — the app handles both (an unconfirmed sign-up says "check your
#     inbox" instead of failing).
#   Authentication → URL Configuration → Site URL: wherever you host the client.
#   Database → Extensions: pg_cron and pg_trgm (the migrations enable them; on a hosted
#     project the toggle is in this screen).
#   Storage: the two buckets arrive from the migrations — nasheed-audio (60 MB, audio/*)
#     and nasheed-artwork (8 MB, image/*), both public.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------- hosting
# Any static host works, because there is no server to run: Vercel, Netlify, Cloudflare
# Pages, Supabase's own static hosting, or `npm run preview` behind nginx. Set the two
# VITE_ variables in the host's environment screen so the build picks them up.
